# Diagnostic evidence and causal localization

Scope: declare a useful span using diagnostic evidence and causal localization.

## When to use this type

An opaque symptom admits multiple causes and evidence must cross a boundary to choose a repair. Bound and sanitize diagnostics; a repaired shared prerequisite permits downstream execution without proving its correctness.

## Information and anchors

Name candidate causes, evidence origin, fields/channel retained, correlation identity and the conclusions each observation supports.

Candidate anchor roles: evidence-producing stage; lossy wrapper; diagnostic schema/consumer; bounded worked decomposition. Select existing content carrying the rule or likely to be encountered at the risky decision. These are roles, not a required anchor count.

## Declare

1. Identify the risky local assumption and its authoritative rule; label inference or unresolved requirements.
2. Inspect existing spans before adding another. Choose anchors that expose the decisive information, including a single-anchor reminder when useful.
3. Select applicable distinguishing evidence: Distinguish compile, launch, runtime and decode failure, or decompose a numerical discrepancy before changing the aggregate formula.
4. Write the rule, scope and evidence limits in the why, then use the declaration recipe in [the skill](../../SKILL.md).

## Illustrative why

> Compiler failures retain stage, status and captured stderr through the runner envelope; solution stdout remains separate from machine results.

This example supplies no authority for another repository. Substitute its actual contract and scope; quote decisive requirement wording where available.

## Adjacent types

Use [observable outcomes](observable-outcome-contracts.md) for the public success/failure contract; use [disambiguating examples](disambiguating-examples-and-counterexamples.md) when the uncertainty is the intended rule rather than the failure cause.

Return to the [type selector](index.md) or [span guidance](../span-guidance.md) for authority, anchor delivery and test-reference rules. The suggested checks require separate execution; a span does not run them.
